{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.saddle_separation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.saddle_separation","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L94-L104","doc":"Saddle separation: under the Vieta relations of two on-curve saddles,\n`x²·(y₁−y₂)² = (x²−3x+1)(x²+x+1)` — the squared saddle separation IS the\n(119) discriminant product, so the branch locus of the Borel-singularity\ndatum `ι̃ ∝ V(σ₁) − V(σ₂)` is exactly the (119) zero set. This is the\nalgebraic core of the substrate-meridian-genericity argument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.sixth_power_at_saddle_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.sixth_power_at_saddle_one","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L89-L92","doc":"The u = 0 saddles are sixth roots of unity: `y² − y + 1 = 0 → y⁶ = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.cube_at_saddle_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.cube_at_saddle_one","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L82-L87","doc":"The u = 0 saddles are primitive sixth roots of unity, cube form:\n`y² − y + 1 = 0 → y³ = −1` (so the saddle values `e^{±iπ/3}` and the\nu = 0 volume datum `Li₂(e^{±iπ/3})` sit at the hexagonal point). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve_root_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve_root_sum","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L75-L80","doc":"Vieta, sum form: `(y₁ − y₂)·(x·(y₁+y₂) − (1 + x − x²)) = curve x y₁ −\ncurve x y₂`; distinct saddles force `x·(y₁+y₂) = 1 + x − x²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve_root_product","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve_root_product","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L67-L73","doc":"Vieta, product form: two on-curve points at the same `x` satisfy\n`(y₁ − y₂)·(x·y₁·y₂ − 1) = 0`; in particular distinct saddles (with `y₁ − y₂`\na nonzerodivisor) force `x·y₁·y₂ = 1`. Stated divisor-free so it holds over\nany commutative ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.gamma_sq_eq_discriminant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.gamma_sq_eq_discriminant","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L59-L65","doc":"The x-cleared Hessian-squared polynomial equals the same (119) product:\n`x⁴ − 2x³ − x² − 2x + 1 = (x²−3x+1)(x²+x+1)` — the amplitude γ² and the\ncurve share their branch locus. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L54-L57","doc":"At `x = 1` the curve reduces to `y² − y + 1` — the u = 0 saddle\nequation (38), whose roots are the primitive sixth roots of unity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.discriminant_factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.discriminant_factorization","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L48-L52","doc":"The y-discriminant of the curve factors as the printed (119) product:\n`(x²−x−1)² − 4x = (x²−3x+1)(x²+x+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.DeformedSaddleCurve.curve","kind":"def","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DeformedSaddleCurve.lean#L43-L46","doc":"The deformed 4₁ saddle curve `x·y² + (x²−x−1)·y + 1` (as an element of\n`R`, vanishing exactly on the curve). Re-derived from the state-integral\nsaddle equation; reduces to `y² − y + 1` at `x = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}